{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The problem\n\n`.github/workflows/test.yml:329-333` currently reads:\n\n```yaml\n  # Windows compatibility (epic 5664 S5). rebar is git- and path-heavy and the gate\n  # runs only Linux + macOS; this sweeps Windows so a path-separator / temp-dir /\n  # file-lock regression surfaces off the hot path. Non-blocking: Windows is not yet a\n  # declared support target, so a genuine incompatibility is a visible finding, never a\n  # red gate.\n```\n\nUnder the operator's adoption of option (a) on `f850-674c-c89f-4b97`, **\"Windows is not yet a\ndeclared support target\" is false.** The tree currently asserts the opposite of the decision, and\nwill keep asserting it for the whole life of this epic unless corrected now.\n\n(The line range was `:340-344` when this ticket was written; re-checked against `origin/main`\n`e510c64dbcb6` at implementation time, the block sits at `:329-333`.)\n\n## Why this is a separate, UNBLOCKED, first-landing task\n\nThis is the one child that is not blocked by S1. It changes no code and no job behaviour — the job\nstays `continue-on-error: true` — so it can land immediately and costs nothing. Its whole value is\nthat it changes what a reader concludes from the red badge: from **\"ignorable, nobody intends to fix\nthis\"** to **\"a known-incomplete declared support target being driven to green under epic\n`simple-brotherly-peacock`\"**.\n\nThat distinction is the entire signal-quality complaint of `f850`. Leaving it unrecorded for months\nwould reproduce the original defect under a new justification.\n\n## Scope — what to change, and what NOT to change\n\n**Do change:** the comment block. It must say Windows IS a declared support target, that the job is\ntemporarily non-voting because the epic is in flight, name the epic and the ADR, and state that the\n`continue-on-error` removal is the epic's exit criterion.\n\n**Also add:** a regression test that fails if the correction is reverted or over-applied. Comments\nsurvive no structural check, so the false-claim half is asserted against the workflow's raw text and\nthe behavioural half against the parsed YAML document — following the anti-rot precedent already in\n`tests/unit/test_probe_wired_into_ci.py`.\n\n**Do NOT change:** `continue-on-error: true`, the `if:` schedule/dispatch condition, or anything in\n`.github/workflows/gerrit-verify.yaml`. Flipping the job to gating now would redden every change\nwith 510 unrelated failures and wreck the `Verified` signal — that is S11's job, at the end, under\nADR 0114.\n\nThis change is additive and text-only: it edits a YAML comment and adds one test file. It touches no\ndata, no store, and no job behaviour, so there is nothing to back up and nothing to roll back beyond\nreverting the commit.\n\n## Testing\n\n```\ngrep -n \"not yet a declared support target\" .github/workflows/test.yml   # must return nothing\ngrep -n \"continue-on-error: true\" .github/workflows/test.yml             # must still match sweep-windows\npython -c \"import yaml; yaml.safe_load(open('.github/workflows/test.yml'))\"\nuv run --no-sync pytest tests/unit/test_windows_support_target.py -q -p no:cacheprovider\nuv run --no-sync pytest -k \"workflow\" -m \"not integration and not external\" -q -p no:cacheprovider\nmake lint && make typecheck\n```\n\nThe new test's own assertions are proven by mutation rather than assumed: reintroduce the falsified\nsentence, delete `continue-on-error: true`, and drop the ADR citation, and confirm each mutation\nfails exactly the intended test and nothing else.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `.github/workflows/test.yml` contains no statement that Windows is not a support target.\n      Proof: `grep -n \"not yet a declared support target\" .github/workflows/test.yml` returns\n      nothing (exit 1).\n- [ ] The `sweep-windows` comment names the ADR, the epic, and the promoting story, and states that\n      removing `continue-on-error` is the exit criterion. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_windows_support_target.py -k cites` passes.\n- [ ] The job's behaviour is unchanged — still `continue-on-error: true` and still restricted to\n      `schedule` / `workflow_dispatch`. Proof:\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_windows_support_target.py -k \"non_voting or hot_path\"` passes, and\n      `git diff origin/main -- .github/workflows/test.yml` shows changes only within comment lines.\n- [ ] `.github/workflows/gerrit-verify.yaml` is not modified. Proof:\n      `git diff --exit-code origin/main -- .github/workflows/gerrit-verify.yaml`.\n- [ ] A regression test exists that fails if the falsified sentence returns or if\n      `continue-on-error: true` is dropped before S11's promotion, and each failure mode is\n      confirmed by mutating the workflow and observing the intended test fail. Proof:\n      `tests/unit/test_windows_support_target.py` exists and the three mutations are recorded as a\n      comment on this ticket.\n- [ ] The repo gates are green. Proof: `make lint` and `make typecheck` both exit 0, with the\n      complexity and mechanism ratchets reporting `new=0 increased=0`.\n"}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788575208606263277,"uuid":"c95e2438-c27d-4edc-a980-a7c8f279a61c"}